<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theophanes the Confessor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 760–<a href="/years/818">818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Theophanes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theophanes the Confessor was a Byzantine monk and chronicler whose Chronographia covers the history of the empire from 284 to 813. It is a key source for the 7th and 8th centuries, including the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. Exiled for opposing iconoclasm, he died on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> and was venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor.jpg" alt="Theophanes the Confessor" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tzortzis Phouka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athonite_Icon_of_Saint_Theophanes_the_Confessor_of_Megalos_Agros_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theophanes was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> of wealthy and noble parents who venerated icons: Isaac, governor of the islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-theophilos">Theodora</a>. His father died when he was three, and Emperor Constantine V saw to his education at the imperial court. He held several offices under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iv-the-khazar">Leo IV the Khazar</a>.</p><p>Married at eighteen, he persuaded his wife to live in virginity, and in 779, after the death of his father-in-law, they separated by mutual consent to take up the religious life. She chose a convent, and he entered the Polychronius Monastery near Cyzicus, then built a monastery on his own lands on the island of Calonymus, where he became skilled at copying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>At the urgent request of his friend George Syncellus, Theophanes continued Syncellus's Chronicle during the years 810 to 815. His part runs from the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> in 284, where Syncellus stopped, to the fall of Michael I Rhangabe in 813. It consists of a year-by-year history and chronological tables, which are full of inaccuracies and may have been filled in by someone else.</p><p>The history reckons by the years of the world and the Christian era and sets out the regnal years of emperors, Persian kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and patriarchs. It is lacking in critical insight, but it surpasses most Byzantine chronicles and preserves accounts of lost authorities for the 7th and 8th centuries, especially from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> onward. Theophanes was the first to claim that Muhammad had epilepsy.</p><p>Cyril Mango has argued that Theophanes contributed little and that most of the contents are the work of Syncellus. The chronicle was much used by later writers, and in 873 to 875 the papal librarian Anastasius made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> compilation from it that made it known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Returning to Sigriano, Theophanes founded an abbey and governed it as abbot, and he attended the Second Council of Nicaea in 787, signing its decrees in defence of the veneration of icons. When Emperor Leo V the Armenian resumed the campaign against icons, he had Theophanes brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and tried in vain to make him condemn the veneration. Theophanes was imprisoned and treated cruelly for two years, then banished to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>, where he lived only seventeen days. Miracles were credited to him after his death, and he is venerated on 12 March in both the Catholic and the Eastern Orthodox churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theophanes the Confessor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Byzantine chroniclers, chiefly <b>Theophanes</b>, say seven tribes there formed a union, though their names are not recorded.</blockquote><a href="/state/seven-slavic-tribes">Seven Slavic Tribes</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Bulgars arrived under Asparuh in 680–681, <b>Theophanes</b> records that the Severi were moved to guard the passes towards Byzantium, while the Seven Slavic Tribes held other borders.</blockquote><a href="/state/severians-balkan">Severians (Balkan)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=797" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Theophanes the Confessor"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=797">What was here in 797 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
